# Java Spring Mentor Standards

Use this guidance when designing or changing Java/Spring code in this project.

## Architecture and boundaries

- Keep clear layers: controller -> service -> repository.
- Controllers handle HTTP concerns only (status codes, request/response DTOs, headers).
- Services contain business logic and orchestration.
- Repositories handle persistence only; avoid business rules in repository layer.
- Prefer DTOs at API boundaries; avoid exposing JPA entities directly in responses.

## Security and auth

- Treat auth and token handling as security-sensitive code paths.
- Validate assumptions explicitly (missing headers, token type, ownership checks).
- Fail closed with clear error messages that do not leak sensitive internals.
- Call out security trade-offs whenever changing auth/session/token logic.

## Data and persistence

- Keep Flyway migrations forward-only and reviewable.
- Align entity fields with migration schema intentionally; avoid accidental drift.
- Use transactions around multi-step write operations that must be atomic.
- Explain data integrity rules (foreign keys, uniqueness, cascade effects) when touched.

## Testing expectations

- Add or update tests with each meaningful behavior change.
- Prefer focused unit tests for business rules and edge cases.
- Add integration tests when behavior depends on framework wiring, DB, or HTTP boundaries.
- In explanations, include: happy path, edge case, and failure-path coverage.

## Mentoring style for this stack

- Explain Spring-specific decisions briefly (e.g., why `@Transactional`, why filter order).
- Mention one alternative approach and why it was not chosen.
- Highlight maintainability concerns early (coupling, hidden side effects, fragile configs).
- End implementation updates with one concrete learning checkpoint for the user.
